1
00:00:12,600 --> 00:00:14,061
Can we track that ball...

2
00:00:14,081 --> 00:00:15,854
without machine learning?

3
00:00:17,150 --> 00:00:19,038
Yes. Using only signals.

4
00:00:19,058 --> 00:00:20,489
Meet FALCON.

5
00:00:21,300 --> 00:00:22,527
You pick the ball.

6
00:00:22,547 --> 00:00:25,054
FALCON looks for that pattern in every frame,

7
00:00:25,074 --> 00:00:26,493
and checks each match.

8
00:00:26,513 --> 00:00:28,401
It locks on and tracks the ball,

9
00:00:28,421 --> 00:00:30,940
until the match gets too messy to trust.

10
00:00:33,400 --> 00:00:34,883
Here's the easy part.

11
00:00:34,903 --> 00:00:36,642
Any picture is just waves,

12
00:00:36,662 --> 00:00:39,339
basic sine and cosine, added together.

13
00:00:39,359 --> 00:00:40,607
Compare the waves,

14
00:00:40,627 --> 00:00:42,570
and the best match lights up.

15
00:00:45,300 --> 00:00:47,167
Then it predicts where to look next,

16
00:00:47,187 --> 00:00:49,024
and verifies the match.

17
00:00:51,450 --> 00:00:53,679
Then a sliding window scans the frame,

18
00:00:53,699 --> 00:00:56,581
tile by tile, until it finds the target.

19
00:00:57,300 --> 00:01:00,319
A new target. A frisbee, thrown toward a dog.

20
00:01:00,339 --> 00:01:02,974
It's small, it spins, and it changes size.

21
00:01:02,994 --> 00:01:04,690
The same method follows it,

22
00:01:04,710 --> 00:01:06,909
until the catch makes it too hard.

23
00:01:07,200 --> 00:01:08,619
No machine learning.

24
00:01:08,639 --> 00:01:13,194
Just signals, a custom F F T, and a Kalman filter, on a C P U.

25
00:01:13,214 --> 00:01:15,733
FALCON, from team Signal Thirteen.
